  "worked_solution": "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\overset{\u2015}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{\u2015}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{\u2015}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{\u2015}{\\lambda })\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{\u2015}{\\lambda }=2-3\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{\u2015}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\u2015}{v} & =[\\begin{aligned}-1-2\\text{i} \\\\ 2-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{\u23df}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{\u2015}{v})}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{\u23df}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{\u2015}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{\u2015}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{\u2015}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
